@startuml stripe-payment-sequence

' Replaces the hand-drawn ASCII diagram in explanation/stripe-payments.md
' ("The shape of it"). Verified against that file,
' App\Actions\Order\CreateOrder, App\Actions\Payment\RecordPayment,
' App\Actions\Payment\CreateStripeIntent,
' App\Http\Controllers\Payment\StripeWebhookController,
' App\Http\Middleware\VerifyStripeWebhookSignature, and
' resources/views/livewire/checkout/checkout-page.blade.php on 2026-09-07.
' Keep the two in sync — the ASCII original documents intent well but this
' is now the maintained source; update this file first.
'
' Every synchronous call RETURNS — the actual object/DTO/status the code
' hands back, not a fire-and-forget arrow — so a reader can see what exists
' after each step, not just infer success. Activation bars mark each
' instance's real lifetime: CheckoutPage stays active across the whole
' request; Webhook activates only for the async delivery it's actually
' processing.

title Stripe payment — the shape of it, with returns and failure paths (explanation/stripe-payments.md)

participant "Cart page" as Cart
participant "CheckoutPage" as Checkout
participant "Stripe" as Stripe
participant "Webhook\n(HandleStripeWebhookEvent)" as Webhook
database "payments /\norders" as DB

Cart -> Checkout : "Checkout"
activate Checkout

group ONE TRANSACTION
  Checkout -> DB : CreateOrder(cart, addresses, carrier)
  activate DB
  DB --> Checkout : Order #1\n(serial=ORD-000001, status=New)
  deactivate DB

  Checkout -> DB : RecordPayment(order, method)
  activate DB
  DB --> Checkout : Payment #1\n(status=Pending, amount=19.90)
  deactivate DB

  Checkout -> Stripe : CreateStripeIntent(payment)
  activate Stripe
  Stripe --> Checkout : <color:green>200</color> Payment #1\n(stripe_payment_intent_id set,\nclient_secret returned)
  deactivate Stripe
end

note over Checkout
  Stripe Elements — card fields served
  by Stripe, never by us. No card data
  reaches this app's servers.
end note

Checkout -> Stripe : confirmPayment(client_secret)
activate Stripe

alt <color:green>card accepted</color>
  Stripe --> Checkout : redirect_status=succeeded\n(browser redirected to\n/checkout/confirmation/1)
  note right of Checkout
    <b>NOT proof of payment.</b>
    The customer can close the tab,
    lose their connection, or never
    see this page — see the webhook
    path below, which is independent.
  end note
else <color:red>card declined / entry error</color>
  Stripe --> Checkout : <color:red>{ error: stripeError }</color>\n(no redirect — stays on /checkout)
  Checkout -> Checkout : stripeError.message shown inline\n(#stripe-error, role="alert") —\ncustomer sees "Your card was declined"\nimmediately, in Stripe's own words
end
deactivate Stripe

deactivate Checkout

...async, on Stripe's own schedule — independent of what the browser did above...

Stripe -> Webhook : POST /stripe/webhook\npayment_intent.succeeded
activate Webhook

alt <color:red>signature missing or invalid</color>
  Webhook --> Stripe : <color:red>400</color> {"error":"Missing signature."}\nor {"error":"Invalid signature."}
  note right of Webhook
    VerifyStripeWebhookSignature runs BEFORE
    the controller. Fails closed — a payment
    can never be marked Paid by a request that
    doesn't verify. Stripe will retry a 400
    with backoff for up to 3 days, same as
    any other non-2xx.
  end note
else <color:orange>webhook secret not configured</color>
  Webhook --> Stripe : <color:orange>500</color> {"error":"Webhook not configured."}
  note right of Webhook
    Deliberately 500, not 400 — the request may
    well be legitimate; this is an ops gap on
    THIS app's side, and 500 tells Stripe to
    keep retrying until it's fixed.
  end note
else <color:green>signature verifies</color>
  Webhook -> DB : insert payment_events row\n(stripe_event_id, UNIQUE)
  activate DB
  DB --> Webhook : PaymentEvent #1\n(id assigned) — or a caught\nUNIQUE violation on redelivery,\ntreated as already-applied, still 200
  deactivate DB

  alt <color:green>processing succeeds</color>
    Webhook -> DB : TransitionPaymentStatus(payment,\nPaid)
    activate DB
    DB --> Webhook : Payment #1\n(status=Paid, paid_at=now())
    deactivate DB
    Webhook --> Stripe : <color:green>200</color> {"received": true}
  else <color:orange>unexpected exception (e.g. DB briefly unavailable)</color>
    Webhook --> Stripe : <color:orange>500</color> {"error":"Processing failed."}
    note right of Webhook
      Retryable on purpose. The PaymentEvent
      insert's own idempotency (the UNIQUE
      constraint above) is what makes a Stripe
      retry of THIS event safe — a second
      delivery re-processing the same
      stripe_event_id is a no-op, not a
      double-apply.
    end note
  end
end

deactivate Webhook

note over Cart, Webhook
  <b>The important asymmetry.</b> The browser's redirect and the
  payment's status update travel on completely different paths.
  The only writer of payments.status is the webhook — never the
  browser-facing confirmation step, and never the "card accepted"
  branch above by itself. A customer who never sees the
  confirmation page can still have a payment that completes
  correctly, once the webhook's own 200 lands.
end note

group cash on delivery (alt path)
  activate Checkout
  Checkout -> DB : CreateOrder(cart, addresses, carrier)
  activate DB
  DB --> Checkout : Order #2\n(serial=ORD-000002, status=New)
  deactivate DB
  Checkout -> DB : RecordPayment(order, COD)
  activate DB
  DB --> Checkout : Payment #2\n(status=Pending, method=COD)
  deactivate DB
  deactivate Checkout
  note over Checkout, Stripe
    No intent, no Elements — straight to
    the confirmation page. CreateStripeIntent
    refuses a COD payment (ADR-0016 names this
    as roughly half the reason to prefer
    Payment Intents: the two paths share
    everything up to the intent step).
  end note
end

@enduml
